# The1 Platform - Project Rules

## Endpoint Organization and Duplication Prevention

- All endpoints must be organized in the correct place in the project tree structure
- Before creating any new endpoints or functions, scan the existing codebase to identify what already exists
- Check existing routes, controllers, services, and utilities before implementing new functionality
- Prefer reusing existing code with small refactors over duplicating functionality
- Follow RESTful API conventions for route organization
- Group related endpoints in appropriate route files
- Maintain consistent naming conventions across all endpoints

## API Standards

- All API endpoints must return JSON responses only
- Use consistent response structure across all endpoints
- Include proper HTTP status codes
- Validate all input data
- Use consistent validation patterns
- Return validation errors in standard format

## Code Documentation

- Always add short description to functions so it be able to easily scan them and know what we have
- Use JSDoc format for function documentation
- Include parameter descriptions and return types
- Keep descriptions concise but informative

## Error Handling and Logging

- Always make sure we are creating endpoints and functions in a way that will not crash the app (try catch for example) with relevant logging
- Use try-catch blocks for all async operations and database calls
- Implement proper error logging with timestamps and context
- Log errors with appropriate levels (error, warn, info, debug)
- Include request ID and user context in error logs
- Never let unhandled errors crash the application
- Return meaningful error responses to clients
- Log all API requests and responses for debugging

## Security

- Implement proper authentication middleware for protected routes
- Validate and sanitize all input data to prevent injection attacks
- Use HTTPS in production environments
- Implement rate limiting to prevent abuse
- Configure CORS properly for client applications
- Never expose sensitive information in error messages
- Use secure session management with proper expiration
- Implement proper password hashing and validation

## Database

- Use MongoDB best practices for schema design
- Implement proper connection handling and error recovery
- Use transactions for critical operations
- Validate data before database operations
- Implement proper indexing for performance
- Use connection pooling for better performance
- Handle database connection errors gracefully
- Implement data validation at the model level

## Testing

- Write unit tests for all utility functions
- Create integration tests for API endpoints
- Test error scenarios and edge cases
- Use the EJS test interface for manual API testing
- Test authentication and authorization flows
- Validate all error responses and status codes
- Test database operations and error handling
- Maintain test coverage for critical functionality

## Environment Configuration

- When relevant and applicable, put all general configuration data in `.env` file
- Use `process.env.<name>` throughout the project to access environment variables
- This enables local development and automatic deployment to AWS
- Never hardcode configuration values in the code
- Use environment-specific values for different deployment stages (dev, staging, prod)
- Always validate first that a parameter does not exist before creating a new one
- Check existing parameters, variables, functions, and objects before adding new ones
- Use descriptive and unique names to avoid conflicts
- Implement proper validation logic for all input parameters
- Return appropriate error messages for invalid or missing parameters

## Project Structure

```
server/
├── .env                 # Environment variables (not version controlled)
├── .env.example         # Example environment file (version controlled)
├── routes/
│   ├── auth.js          # Authentication endpoints
│   ├── users.js         # User management endpoints
│   ├── locations.js     # Location CRUD endpoints
│   ├── events.js        # Event CRUD endpoints
│   └── test.js          # Test interface routes
├── controllers/
├── services/
├── middleware/
├── models/
├── utils/
└── views/
```

## UI/UX Standards

- Never use emoji icons or symbols in the user interface
- Use clean, professional text-based navigation and headers
- Maintain consistent typography and spacing
- Focus on readability and accessibility
- Use color coding and visual hierarchy instead of emojis for categorization
- Keep the interface minimal and professional

## Implementation Process

1. Search existing codebase for similar functionality
2. Check if existing code can be reused or refactored
3. Identify the correct location in project structure
4. Plan implementation following established patterns
5. Implement with proper error handling and validation
6. Update documentation and API specifications
7. Test functionality through EJS test interface
